A||N||8||stimpy||High oil pressure||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||11-11-2000||06:20 PM||||Ever since I had my engine (2.8) rebuilt, my oil pressure guage reads around 80 on start up. Eventually this will go down after it warms up, but I am concerned if this might be a problem for it. Recently when my folks were out, they mentioned that it appears to be burning a small amout of oil in the exhuast. Could this be attributed to that high oil pressure when cold, or do I have bigger fish to fry? <BR>The engine is still under warranty through February, so if I have problems, I need to know quick so I can get something done. Help mr. Wizard!||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||11-11-2000||06:35 PM||||Seems like my 1986 SE V6 (bought brand new) had that sort of oil pressure behavior from the start - 80psi even at idle, until it warmed up.  BTW with 14 years on it, I <B>wish</B> it had that kind of oil pressure!<BR> <BR>Could the slight oil burning in the exhaust, be just what you get until the rings seat themselves?  That's what they used to say, anyway...  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||11-11-2000||06:40 PM||||I hope so, but after 8000 miles I was hoping that the rings and all would be seated by now.  Oh BTW, if it makes any difference regarding my question, I use Mobil 1 5-30. FROM WAL MART, HAHAHA!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-11-2000||07:43 PM||||You didn't put synthetic in it right after the rebuild did you? That has been known to keep rings from seating right.<P>Any oil burning at 8000 would be cause for concern I'd think. Even with synthetic oil they should have seated by now. I think you need to be talking to the builder.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||11-11-2000||07:48 PM||||When I put the 3.4 in my Fiero, I swapped in a new stock replacement oil pump, not a high volume. The engine has 75,000 miles on it and it will hold 80 psi when cold and 60 at normal engine temp at highway speeds. It sounds like you have other problems. Try some full throttle accelerations to about 4,000 and let off the throttle a few times. The high load/high vac. might help finish getting the rings seated. Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||11-11-2000||07:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>You didn't put synthetic in it right after the rebuild did you? That has been known to keep rings from seating right.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ooops, Yeah, I kinda did. At 800 miles I started her on synthetic. Should I swap back to plain old Valvoline for a while or what? Now, I'm not blowing massive smokestacks or anything, just a little during warm-up. What should I have the builder look for?  This would have nothing to do with the engine overheating, would it? (Not that I think it ever did, but my temp gauge does not work and that may give me warranty problems.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||11-11-2000||08:00 PM||||High reading oil pressure is usually caused by the oil sending unit.  I've replaced mine 3x in 13 years on my 87 GT.  My experience is that it reads about 80 on start up and eventually drops to 60 once warmed up.  High bias.  When the sending unit fails, the gauge pegs and doesn't drop back until the car is shut off.  The replacement of the sending unit is easy.  The design of the sending unit was nodified for the 88  2.8 engine.  Still not the best design but the gauge doesn't peg and reads typically in the 30-50 psi.||||reg||3
Z||000007||fiero56||11-11-2000||09:52 PM||||cro-mo rings have also been know to take quite a while to seat.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||11-11-2000||10:21 PM||||I just finished putting a 12,000 mile engine in my 87 GT, and I was surprised to see the oil pressure at 40 psi Idle, and pegged at highway speeds. I just figured the sender is bad.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
